WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.009
The internet - and YouTube in particular -
offers a vast ocean of content, and if

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:09.660
you don't want to get lost or waste time,
you have to learn how to navigate it. Hi

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.590
My name is Luca from Luca from LucaLampariello.com, and today I'm going to show you how

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:18.570
I use the Internet, and in particular
YouTube, to learn and improve my foreign

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:25.289
languages. Now, specifically for
deliberate language learning, there are

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:30.420
three criteria that are very good when
it comes to choosing your content. The

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:36.000
first criterion is that you should abide
by content that is interesting to you. If

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.160
something is interesting, your main focus
is not the language itself, but it's the

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.450
message and as a consequence you will
learn the language that is used to

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:50.219
convey that message. The second crucial
factor or point is that this content

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:54.210
should be comprehensible to you. So, you
don't want to choose content that has

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:58.710
maybe just audio or in which the
audio is too fast and is not good

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.600
quality, but instead you want to focus on
content that is very clear, that is

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.939
possibly made for learners, but not
necessarily, and that has subtitles in

00:01:08.939 --> 00:01:13.110
your target language and in English. The
third important point to keep in mind

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:18.840
for what I'm going to tell you later is
that the video be a maximum of five-six

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.159
minutes. In fact, if the video is short,
you can focus on it better, and you can

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:26.939
do a number of repetitions on it which
are going to expand the amount of time

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:31.860
that you're going to spend on the video.
And if the video is, say, ten minutes, then

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.119
it will take you 1 hour to do that so..
keep it short, keep it sweet

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:37.600
and keep it effective.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:48.000
So, one of the resources that you can
find on YouTube which satisfies the

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.659
aforementioned criteria is easy language.
Easy Hungarian, easy French, easy Italian

00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:58.050
you name it!
There are multiple versions for multiple

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:02.280
languages, and for multiple learners. In
particular, I have been learning

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.420
Hungarian for quite some time , and I still
use easy Hungarian as a wonderful

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:12.900
learning resource. So now let me show you directly how I do it on my computer. So,

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.130
one recommendation as you can see here
on the screen: you have the box of the

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:22.230
video itself, but you also have to
consider that there's this column on the

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:27.090
right that shows you the other related
videos. A very good recommendation for

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.290
avoiding distractions is to put it in
full-screen because what happens with

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.790
YouTube is that you have a lot of other
videos possibly that you can watch, but you

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.209
have to pay attention because those are
distracting. The moment you start

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:42.870
watching the video, your eye falls on the
other videos as well, and you want to

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:46.829
change the video even before you finish
it, so make sure that you have it on full

00:02:46.829 --> 00:02:53.040
screen. So as you can see, we are in full
screen now and the presenter Justyna is

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.670
holding a microphone and she's going to
go around interviewing people on the

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:00.959
street, which allows you to actually
enjoy how people really talk. We're in

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:06.870
Budapest here and this video is easy
Hungarian -  let's get started.. oh one

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:11.430
second before we start - an important
thing is that as you can see, I have my

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:17.220
piece of paper here to write the
title of the video. So, you have here easy

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:23.970
Hungarian, and then the title of episode
1 and then to add the date this is

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:28.350
important for your episodic memory and
for your motivation, and I'm going to

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:35.459
talk about this a little bit later. So,
let's get started.

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:39.269
(Speaking Hungarian)

00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:44.770
(Speaking Hungarian)

00:03:46.599 --> 00:03:50.000
(Speaking Hungarian)

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:58.150
So the very first step that I do do is very simple: I watch the video, I enjoy the video, and I look at the subtitles.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:02.349
As you can see, you have subtitles, both
in Hungarian and in English, which makes

00:04:02.349 --> 00:04:07.209
this video comprehensible and very nice
to look at but the first thing that I do

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:12.459
a step number one is to watch this video
in Hungarian and with subtitles in

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:17.829
Hungarian, and why do I do that? Well
because I focus on the video itself, on

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:21.459
the content itself. It does not matter
how much I understand, I'm focusing more

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:26.700
on the relationship between the spoken
language and the written language.

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:41.139
So, here they're talking and you know about
somewhere do you want to travel etc and

00:04:41.139 --> 00:04:48.190
I finish watching the entire video once.
Then, step number two is to rewind the

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:53.860
video as I'm "rewinding" it right now and
to watch it by focusing this time on the

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:59.080
English subtitles so let me watch this
again this time looking directly at the

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.100
English subtitles below.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.909
Okay, after watching in a second time, but this

00:05:12.909 --> 00:05:17.949
time with the English subtitles, I have
started understanding more and more of

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:22.509
the content because this is content for
I would say intermediate-advanced

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:25.930
learners, it really shows how people talk
on the streets these people were getting

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:29.500
interviewed they don't know that they're
doing this content creating content for

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.639
learners so they talk very fast and they
use expressions that are commonly used

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:39.009
so it's normal that I will miss a lot of
stuff. So, after step 2, I already got a

00:05:39.009 --> 00:05:43.750
better idea of the content but I still
miss a few things so you want to do

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:48.520
something else and it is to watch it
again watching it with Hungarian

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:54.400
subtitles. So you're integrating the whole thing and you're getting the whole framework, or the whole picture

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:03.510
(Speaking Hungarian)

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:07.500
(Speaking Hungarian)

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:13.320
Ok, so, you have watched it three times. Now, one more thing that I do that does make a

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:19.440
difference is that I jot things down. I
always say.. you know, the Romans used to

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.210
say "Verba volant sed scripta manent" and I can't stress this enough, if you

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:30.840
want things to stick, use a piece of
paper or find somehow some way to save

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:36.080
the words or the expressions or the
information somehow on a piece of paper.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:42.870
So what I do is I watch the video for
the fourth time but this time I'm going

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:48.690
to stop it here and there and I'm going
to jot down and mark and then look up

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:54.000
the words that I find interesting
related to this video. So, let me show how I do it

00:06:54.400 --> 00:07:01.000
(Speaking Hungarian)

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:10.100
So I go back, I found a word that was interesting there, "fordítani",

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:16.250
which is "translating" - which is word I
love - and I can see directly the

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:22.140
translation - no pun intended -
below I've neat I'd need the internet to

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:26.300
translate this so forditani

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:43.590
So, another thing that I do and I want
you to look at my position is that this

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:49.230
configuration allows me to easily use my
left hand to stop the video, and my right

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:53.670
hand to write directly, so I just need to
stop here and write it, and you want

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.490
to make things easier for yourself
because if you're in an uncomfortable

00:07:56.490 --> 00:08:00.450
position of your body, then you have to
change the position of your body, you

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:05.130
have to stop, you have to go back..this
way, I just use left hand right hand it's

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:09.100
really straightforward and it goes
really fast

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.750
Okay so I've done step number one, step

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:42.339
number two, step number three, step number four, and we are at step number five.

00:08:42.339 --> 00:08:47.350
If you want, and some things are still not
clear, the last thing you can do is just

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:51.730
to relax now - now you got the content,
because you've seen multiple times in

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:55.899
different versions - you're adding variety
to your brain and each step serves a

00:08:55.899 --> 00:09:00.100
specific purpose, now I just watch the
video again you can choose which

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:04.389
subtitles or you can even watch it
without looking at the subtitles to see

00:09:04.389 --> 00:09:09.129
how much you understand but the idea is
that now you can relax and look at the

00:09:09.129 --> 00:09:13.120
full content and focus on the message
and this is a very straightforward way

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:18.730
to look and extract information from a
very simple video which you can see is

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:24.610
four minutes it took me 30 minutes to
complete it, 25-30 minutes, and that's the

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:29.500
reason why I was stressing the fact that
the length be 5 minutes or 3 minutes or

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:33.759
4 minutes because if you decide to
tackle a 10 minute video, then this

00:09:33.759 --> 00:09:38.110
exercise or training or whatever you
want to call it, is going to become very

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:42.040
very long. You want to keep it smooth, you want to keep it efficient, and you want

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:45.059
to keep it smart.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:52.240
So, I've gone through these five steps
and that's great! I'm satisfied, I've put

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.870
in the work. But the truth is that my
learning session is not finished yet

00:09:55.870 --> 00:10:00.460
there is one last point that makes a
difference. What I do is I make sure that

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:06.160
every time I finish a learning session, I
first put an entry into my logbook which

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:12.400
is in a Google Docs or online and on top
of that I also make sure that I save

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:17.170
these pieces of paper into a folder. I'm
going to give it a label - Hungarian or

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:21.790
easy Hungarian - and then every single
time that I finish, I put this piece of

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:26.170
paper into the folder. The reason why I
do that is because a folder is a

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:30.010
physical entity that you can actually
see and every time that you start

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:34.780
learning or finish learning a session
of Hungarian or any other language for

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:38.350
that matter, I'm constantly reminded of
the amazing amount of work that I've

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:43.990
been putting every single day. So, if you
want to use YouTube as a fantastic

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:47.830
learning tool to learn a bunch of
languages, you want to keep in mind the

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:53.800
following: first, you want to find content
that is short, comprehensible and

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:59.350
interesting to you. Second, make sure that
you break down your learning session

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:04.030
into multiple steps in which each step
serves a specific purpose and make sure

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:08.890
that you jot things down and you have a
good body configuration or body position

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:13.540
so that you can do multiple things
smoothly at the same time. And third, make

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:17.530
sure that you store the information and
every time you finish a learning session

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:22.210
you jot down the entry on your logbook
and you keep a piece of paper and you

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:26.920
accumulate pieces of paper into a
physical folder that reminds you of the

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:31.000
amount of work you put every single day. So there you have it. If you want to

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.460
learn how to use YouTube to learn or
improve a bunch of languages, please

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:40.600
don't forget to come to my website where
I have written an extensive article

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:45.100
about this very same matter, you can find
a lot of resources, a lot of other links

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:49.630
and there's going to be a lot of new
surprises in the next future, so stay

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:53.440
tuned, subscribe to my blog, subscribe to
my channel if you haven't done so and

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:57.870
good language learning and see you very
soon!

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:21.339
Oh and one last thing: if you have any
comments or questions, please write them

00:12:21.339 --> 00:12:24.819
below because I'm going to take the most
